WRITTEN ARGUMENTS ON BEHALF OF THE ACCUSED

RESPECTFULLY SUBMITTED:

I. MOTIVE

The prosecution alleges motive in the form of a long-standing land dispute. However, motive alone cannot establish guilt. Even if there was a dispute, it does not prove that the applicants committed the murder. The dispute was civil in nature and pending before appropriate authorities.

II. LAST SEEN EVIDENCE

The last-seen theory relies entirely on PW-2, whose statement is contradictory. The time mentioned in the 161 statement does not match the time alleged in the FIR. There is a material discrepancy of over one hour. Such contradiction renders the last-seen evidence unreliable.

III. DYING DECLARATION

The alleged dying declaration suffers from multiple infirmities:
- It was not recorded by a Magistrate
- There is no medical certificate of fitness
- It is oral and not corroborated by any reliable evidence
- It was allegedly made to the wife, who is an interested party

The Supreme Court has repeatedly held that oral dying declarations recorded by non-Magistrates without fitness certificates are viewed with great suspicion.

IV. MEDICAL EVIDENCE

The post-mortem report shows multiple blunt force injuries. However, the nature of injuries does not conclusively establish the weapon used. The injuries could have been caused by any blunt object, not necessarily the wooden stick allegedly recovered.

V. FSL EVIDENCE

The FSL report claims blood match with the deceased. However, the chain of custody was broken for 48 hours. The weapon was allegedly recovered on 16.03.2025 but sent to FSL only on 18.03.2025 without any explanation for the delay. Such lapse fatally affects the credibility of the FSL report.

VI. RECOVERY OF WEAPON

The recovery of the wooden stick is hit by Section 27 principles. There were no independent witnesses to the recovery. The seizure memo was prepared in a suspicious manner. The principles laid down in State vs. Kathikalu are not satisfied.

VII. CIRCUMSTANTIAL EVIDENCE

For conviction based on circumstantial evidence, the prosecution must establish a complete chain of circumstances that excludes every hypothesis of innocence. In the present case, the chain is broken at multiple links. The circumstances do not unequivocally point to the guilt of the applicants.

VIII. BENEFIT OF DOUBT

When two views are possible, the view favourable to the accused must be adopted. The prosecution evidence is weak, contradictory, and suffers from serious procedural lapses. The benefit of doubt must go to the applicants.

PRAYER

It is therefore prayed that the applicants be discharged / acquitted as the case may be.

